sections.say:
"The three-line version is:

```
const res = await fetch(url);
const body = await res.json();
return body.results.map(u => ({ ... }));
```

That's not wrong so much as unguarded, in three specific ways.

One: no status check. `fetch` only rejects on a network-level failure — DNS, connection refused, CORS. A 404 or a 500 resolves perfectly happily with `ok: false`, so this code sails past the error and tries to `.map` over whatever the error page's body parsed to. Usually that's `body.results` being undefined and a TypeError three lines later that points at the wrong place.

Two: no defense on optional fields, so one record missing `location` takes down the whole transform.

Three: the reshape is inline at the call site, so every caller re-derives the wire format. I'd rather have one function that owns the boundary."

examples.trace:
res.ok true, status 200 -> guard passes
body.results = 4 records
filter(active) -> [1 Ada, 3 Grace, 4 Linus]   (2 Alan dropped)
map:
  Ada   -> { id:1, fullName:'Ada Lovelace',   city:'London',   tagCount:2 }
  Grace -> { id:3, fullName:'Grace Hopper',   city:'New York', tagCount:3 }
  Linus -> location missing -> ?. short-circuits to undefined -> ?? 'unknown'
           tags missing     -> ?. short-circuits to undefined -> ?? 0
        -> { id:4, fullName:'Linus Torvalds', city:'unknown',  tagCount:0 }
sort by tagCount desc -> [Grace(3), Ada(2), Linus(0)] ✓

await fetch(url) -> RESOLVES (not rejects) with { ok: false, status: 503 }
if (!res.ok) -> true -> throw new Error('HTTP 503')
res.json() is never called -- we bail before downloading the body
caller's try/catch sees 'HTTP 503' ✓

Contrast, without the guard:
  body = await res.json()       // parses the error page, maybe {} or {error:...}
  body.results.filter(...)      // TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined
  ...and the stack points at the transform, not at the failed request.

reference.code:
// The API's shape is not our shape. Keep the reshape in ONE place so the
// rest of the app never sees the wire format.
async function loadActiveUsers(fetchImpl, url) {
  const res = await fetchImpl(url);
  // fetch does NOT reject on 404/500 -- only on network failure. Guard it.
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);
  // Reading the body is a SECOND await: the first resolved on headers alone.
  const body = await res.json();
  return body.results
    .filter(u => u.active)
    .map(u => ({
      id: u.id,
      fullName: `${u.name.first} ${u.name.last}`,
      city: u.location?.city ?? 'unknown',
      tagCount: u.tags?.length ?? 0,
    }))
    .sort((a, b) => b.tagCount - a.tagCount);
}

